+# Hegel property-based testing report: `loro`
+
+Property-based tests written with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest` 0.28.2)
+against the public `loro` crate API. All tests live in
+`crates/loro/tests/loro_rust_test.rs`, module `hegel_pbt`.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+Ten properties (each its own test), plus two pinned known-failure tests
+documenting real bugs found (see Suspected bugs).
+
+1. **`prop_concurrent_replicas_converge` (crown property).** 2–4 replicas with
+   distinct peer ids apply up to 40 randomly drawn edits across five root
+   containers (text, list, movable list, map, counter), with drawn *partial
+   syncs* interleaved mid-stream (drawn direction and drawn transport:
+   updates-since-vv / all-updates / full snapshot). After a full mesh exchange
+   (every ordered pair, in a drawn order, two passes) all replicas must have
+   deep-equal `get_deep_value()`, equal `oplog_vv()`, and equal
+   `oplog_frontiers()`. Evidence: convergence is the core CRDT contract; the
+   upstream fuzzer (`crates/fuzz/src/crdt_fuzzer.rs::check_equal`) asserts the
+   same post-sync deep-value equality.
+2. **`prop_snapshot_modes_preserve_state`.** Every snapshot-flavoured export
+   mode (`Snapshot`, `ShallowSnapshot` at current frontiers, `StateOnly(None)`,
+   `SnapshotAt` at current frontiers), imported into a fresh doc (or via
+   `LoroDoc::from_snapshot`), reproduces the exporter's deep value; full
+   snapshots also reproduce `oplog_vv`/`oplog_frontiers`. Evidence: doc
+   comments on `ExportMode` (`crates/loro-internal/src/encoding.rs`) and the
+   fuzzer's `StateOnlyRoundTrip` action. Shallow modes are excluded when the
+   frontiers have ≥ 3 tips — that is bug #2 below, pinned separately.
+3. **`prop_all_updates_roundtrip`.** `export(all_updates())` imported into a
+   fresh doc reproduces deep value, version vector, and frontiers. Evidence:
+   `ExportMode::Updates` docs ("contains all the history").
+4. **`prop_import_is_idempotent`.** Importing the same blob (updates or
+   snapshot) 1–3 extra times into a target (fresh, or with its own
+   disjoint-peer history) changes nothing: deep value, vv, and frontiers are
+   identical after the first import. Evidence: CRDT idempotence contract;
+   `ImportStatus` distinguishes already-known ranges.
+5. **`prop_text_matches_string_model`** (state machine). `LoroText` against a
+   `Vec<char>` model: insert/delete in unicode, UTF-8, and UTF-16 index
+   spaces (offsets computed per-space from the model), `splice` (also asserts
+   the returned deleted string), `push_str`, `char_at`, `slice`; invariant
+   checks `to_string`, `len_unicode`, `len_utf8`, `len_utf16`. Also asserts
+   misaligned UTF-8 insert offsets return `Err` (per AGENTS.md: "invalid
+   external input should return `Err`"). Characters drawn from the full
+   codepoint range so the three index spaces genuinely differ.
+6. **`prop_list_matches_vec_model`** (state machine). `LoroList` vs
+   `Vec<LoroValue>`: insert/delete/push/clear, `pop` agreement, `get`
+   agreement; invariant compares `to_vec`, `len`, `is_empty`.
+7. **`prop_movable_list_matches_vec_model`** (state machine). `LoroMovableList`
+   vs `Vec<LoroValue>`, adding `set` and `mov` (modelled as remove+insert,
+   matching the existing `movable_list` unit test's examples).
+8. **`prop_map_matches_hashmap_model`** (state machine). `LoroMap` vs
+   `HashMap<String, LoroValue>` with a small key pool (to force overwrites)
+   plus arbitrary keys: insert, delete-existing, delete-arbitrary (missing-key
+   delete is `Ok` per `MapHandler::delete`), `get` agreement; invariant
+   compares the JSON projection, `len`, `is_empty`.
+9. **`prop_checkout_reproduces_recorded_states`.** Build a (possibly
+   multi-peer) doc, then record `(state_frontiers, deep value)` checkpoints
+   after each of up to 15 further edits; `checkout` to drawn checkpoints must
+   reproduce the recorded value; `checkout_to_latest` restores the final value
+   and re-attaches. Evidence: `checkout`/`checkout_to_latest` docs; the
+   fuzzer's `check_history`.
+10. **`prop_frontiers_vv_roundtrip`.** For a doc with merged multi-peer
+    history: `frontiers_to_vv(oplog_frontiers()) == Some(oplog_vv())` and
+    `vv_to_frontiers(oplog_vv()) == oplog_frontiers()`. Evidence:
+    `frontiers_to_vv`/`vv_to_frontiers` docs describe them as the two
+    version representations of the same point in the DAG.
+
+Generator notes: values avoid `f64` payloads (NaN/`-0.0` would make equality
+oracles unsound) but use full-range `i64`, booleans, null, and full-Unicode
+strings; counter increments are integer-valued so replica sums are exact in
+any application order (a bound protecting the test oracle, not the library).
+
+## Test results
+
+- `cargo test -p loro --test loro_rust_test`: **187 passed, 2 failed
+  (the two pinned known-bug tests), 1 ignored** — all pre-existing tests and
+  all 10 properties pass; the 2 failures are deliberate pins of real bugs.
+- Liveness check performed: sabotaging the list model (extra phantom push)
+  produced a counterexample shrunk to a single `push` step; sabotage reverted.
+- Exploratory 10× run (`test_cases = 1000` on all 10 properties): no new
+  failures; committed tests use the default case count.
+- The two pinned tests fail deterministically on every run (first generated
+  case), including from a clean `.hegel` state.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### Bug 1: replica state depends on import batching (counter container never materializes on zero-sum diffs)
+
+Pinned by `prop_counter_zero_sum_import_batching_diverges_known_bug`.
+
+Minimal counterexample:
+
+```rust
+let origin = LoroDoc::new();           // peer 1
+let c = origin.get_counter("counter");
+c.increment(1.0).unwrap(); origin.commit();
+c.decrement(1.0).unwrap(); origin.commit();
+
+// replica importing the two changes as TWO blobs -> {"counter": 0.0}
+// replica importing the same two changes as ONE blob -> {}
+// both replicas have identical oplog version vectors
+```
+
+Code path: `CounterDiffCalculator::calculate_diff`
+(`crates/loro-internal/src/diff_calc/counter.rs`) returns the *net sum* of the
+imported range as `InternalDiff::Counter(f64)`;
+`InternalDiff::is_empty` (`crates/loro-internal/src/event.rs`, `Counter(c) =>
+c.abs() < f64::EPSILON`) declares a zero-sum diff empty; and
+`DiffCalculator::calc_diff_internal` (`crates/loro-internal/src/diff_calc.rs`,
+`if !diff.is_empty() || bring_back`) then drops it entirely, so the receiving
+replica never creates the container. Snapshot imports and locally-originated
+edits do create it, so identical histories render differently depending on
+delivery batching. `checkout` uses the same diff path, so time travel across a
+zero-sum span exhibits the same asymmetry. A single `increment(0.0)` (an op
+the API accepts) triggers it too, as would net sums smaller than
+`f64::EPSILON`. The upstream fuzzer masks this class: its `assert_value_eq`
+(`crates/fuzz/src/actor.rs`) compares missing keys against `Double(0.)` and
+skips empty containers.
+
+Sibling properties stay green by materializing the same root containers on
+every replica (`touch_roots`), which makes container *presence* symmetric; the
+value itself converges because integer-valued sums are only dropped when the
+net change is zero.
+
+### Bug 2: shallow snapshot of ≥ 3 concurrent tips exports successfully but cannot be imported
+
+Pinned by `prop_shallow_snapshot_of_concurrent_tips_is_importable_known_bug`.
+
+Minimal counterexample:
+
+```rust
+// peer 1: insert 1 char, delete it  (tip 1@1, change has 2 ops)
+// peer 2: insert 1 char             (tip 0@2)
+// peer 3: insert 1 char             (tip 0@3)
+// merge everything into peer 1's doc, then:
+let bytes = d1.export(ExportMode::shallow_snapshot(&d1.oplog_frontiers())).unwrap(); // Ok!
+LoroDoc::new().import(&bytes)  // Err(SwitchToVersionBeforeShallowRoot)
+```
+
+Code path: `calc_shallow_doc_start`
+(`crates/loro-internal/src/encoding/shallow_snapshot.rs:318`) reduces the
+target frontiers by iterated *pairwise* common ancestors. With frontier order
+`[0@2, 0@3, 1@1]`, the concurrent pair `(0@2, 0@3)` has an empty GCA and
+contributes nothing, while the odd tip out `1@1` is carried over verbatim, so
+the loop converges to the single frontier `{1@1}` — which does **not**
+dominate the other tips (the intended fallback "empty frontiers = full
+history" in the same function is never reached because `next != current` and
+`len == 1`). The exported snapshot is shallow-rooted at a version concurrent
+with retained ops; importing it fails in `_checkout_without_emitting`
+(`crates/loro-internal/src/loro.rs:1696`,
+`oplog.dag.is_before_shallow_root(frontiers)`). The trigger needs the
+surviving tip's change to reference earlier ops on its own peer (counter > 0);
+with the root at a counter-0 tip the effective start vv is empty and the
+export degrades to full history, which is why 3 single-op peers don't fail.
+`StateOnly` exports share `calc_shallow_doc_start` and are excluded the same
+way in the sibling property. The upstream fuzzer never sees this because it
+discards shallow export/import errors (`let _ = ...export(...)`,
+`if let Ok(bytes) = ...` in `crates/fuzz/src/crdt_fuzzer.rs`).
+
+Either the export should fall back to full history (per its own comment) or it
+should return an error — succeeding and producing unimportable bytes can lose
+the document.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Deep-value equality without root-container normalization.** First drafts
+  compared raw `get_deep_value()` between replicas/roundtrips. Unsound:
+  merely *accessing* a root container (`get_text(...)`, or a no-op edit like
+  `text.insert(0, "")`) registers an empty container in the local state
+  without creating any op, so it exists locally but never syncs; containers
+  are also never removed from state on checkout-to-before-creation. This is
+  documented behavior (`set_hide_empty_root_containers`; the upstream fuzzer
+  normalizes empty containers away). Fixed by materializing the same fixed
+  root set on every compared doc (`touch_roots`) rather than weakening the
+  comparison — this keeps full-strength equality on everything else.
+- **Float-valued map/list payloads.** Dropped `f64` from `draw_value`:
+  `NaN != NaN` and `-0.0 == 0.0` make both `LoroValue` equality and JSON
+  projections unreliable oracles. Integer/string/bool/null payloads keep the
+  oracles exact. (Counter is inherently `f64`; integer-valued increments keep
+  sums exact under reordering.)
+- **`vv_to_frontiers` on arbitrary version vectors** was considered and not
+  asserted — only vvs that correspond to actual DAG points (the doc's own
+  `oplog_vv`) are claimed to roundtrip; arbitrary vvs have no such contract.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The workflow's "read the crate's own fuzz targets" advice was decisive
+  twice: `crates/fuzz` revealed both the intended equality semantics
+  (`assert_value_eq`'s normalizations, which explained "failures" that were
+  documented behavior) and the places upstream silently tolerates errors
+  (shallow export), which is exactly where a real bug was hiding.
+- "Run and Reflect" separation of *real bug* vs *unsound property* vs *too
+  broad generator* fit this session perfectly: 4 initial failures decomposed
+  into 2 real bugs (pinned), 1 unsound-oracle issue (empty-root
+  normalization), and 0 generator problems.
+- The Rust reference's state-machine docs were accurate and sufficient
+  (rules with `tc.assume`, invariants, `hegel::stateful::run`); `Pool` wasn't
+  needed. One gap: no guidance on *multi-replica* stateful tests — a state
+  machine holding N docs with sync rules would have been a natural fit for
+  the convergence property, but it wasn't clear whether invariants that only
+  hold *after* quiescence (full sync) can be expressed, so a plain imperative
+  test was used instead. A documented pattern for "converges after
+  stabilization" properties would help.
+- Deterministic pinning guidance ("boost the generator toward the
+  counterexample region") worked: both pinned tests fail on every case by
+  construction while still drawing payloads.
+- Minor: replayed `.hegel` failure DB entries plus in-flight generator edits
+  were briefly confusing, exactly as the reference's gotcha #11 warns —
+  the warning was accurate and useful.
